WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.442
A single long lived application key stored
beside every encrypted record

2
00:00:04.442 --> 00:00:08.563
creates a broad point of exposure and a
difficult migration problem.

3
00:00:08.563 --> 00:00:12.236
Anyone who obtains both can decrypt the
protected data, and

4
00:00:12.236 --> 00:00:15.640
changing that key can affect every record
at once.

5
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.507
Separating keys from ciphertext means
controlling their access

6
00:00:18.507 --> 00:00:21.463
and lifecycle independently, not merely
putting them in

7
00:00:21.463 --> 00:00:24.509
different columns or encoding the key
before storage.

8
00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:26.480
Envelope encryption introduces two roles.

9
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.064
A data encryption key, or DEK, encrypts
application data.

10
00:00:30.064 --> 00:00:33.110
A key encryption key, or KEK, protects
that

11
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.514
data key using an established wrapping or
encryption mechanism.

12
00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:39.560
The application stores ciphertext, the
wrapped data key,

13
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.606
and the approved metadata required to
interpret both.

14
00:00:42.606 --> 00:00:45.831
The wrapped key may accompany the
ciphertext because recovering

15
00:00:45.831 --> 00:00:49.236
its plaintext requires separate authority
to use the KEK.

16
00:00:49.236 --> 00:00:53.625
In a common workflow, a managed service
generates or protects a data

17
00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:57.746
key while the long lived KEK remains
inside controlled key infrastructure.

18
00:00:57.746 --> 00:01:00.255
The application uses the plaintext data
key

19
00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:03.301
locally, stores only its protected form,
and later

20
00:01:03.301 --> 00:01:05.988
requests unwrapping when authorized
processing requires decryption.

21
00:01:05.988 --> 00:01:09.393
Plaintext data keys therefore commonly
exist in application memory.

22
00:01:09.393 --> 00:01:12.797
Keep their exposure short, avoid
unnecessary copies, and never

23
00:01:12.797 --> 00:01:16.470
write them to logs, configuration,
databases, or temporary files.

24
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.785
Managed runtimes may retain copies, so
releasing a reference

25
00:01:19.785 --> 00:01:23.189
is not proof that every byte has been
erased.

26
00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:26.504
Separate or scoped data keys can reduce
shared exposure,

27
00:01:26.504 --> 00:01:29.371
while managed key services or hardware
security modules

28
00:01:29.371 --> 00:01:32.775
provide controlled use and audit records
for root keys.

29
00:01:32.775 --> 00:01:36.448
Where the format and service permit,
rewrapping data keys can

30
00:01:36.448 --> 00:01:40.211
change the KEK protecting them without
re-encrypting all bulk data.

31
00:01:40.211 --> 00:01:42.451
This is an architectural benefit, not

32
00:01:42.451 --> 00:01:45.138
an automatic property of every encrypted
record.

33
00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:48.184
Envelope encryption still requires correct
algorithms, nonce rules,

34
00:01:48.184 --> 00:01:51.051
context binding, service authorization,
and failure handling.

35
00:01:51.051 --> 00:01:53.201
The hierarchy makes those responsibilities
more

36
00:01:53.201 --> 00:01:55.620
manageable; it does not remove them.

